window.docContents[33].resolve({"/Abstraction/Function-and-program/#Core-C______--Abstraction--Function-and-program":{"contents":"A function is well typed when its body is well typed under the context of its parameters and its result type. Its result type is void or storable, each parameter type is bindable, and every type is well formed. A reference parameter may thus have an object type.\n\nA program is a list of declarations of classes and functions with a function int main(). The initial store is empty, because there are no global variables, and the result is the value of main().Two functions of one name have distinct and distinguishable signatures, . Each class  is well formed,  of . Each class template a header declares has a module of , and each function it declares has one that agrees with the declaration.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Abstraction","header":"5.5. Function and program","id":"/Abstraction/Function-and-program/#Core-C______--Abstraction--Function-and-program"},"/Design-restrictions/Constructs-left-out/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions--Constructs-left-out":{"contents":"Core C++ leaves out exceptions, function templates, partial specialisation, objects by value, copy constructors, RAII, multiple inheritance, arrays and separate compilation.Arrays violate the type completeness principle of Watt (2004, §2.5.3), since C++17 restricts the operations on them. An array is not assignable. A parameter of array type is adjusted to a pointer (N4659 §11.3.5 ¶5), and no function returns an array (N4659 §11.3.5 ¶10). An array converts to a pointer to its first element (N4659 §7.2). The subscript a[i] is *(a + i) (N4659 §8.2.1 ¶1), and an index outside the array is undefined (N4659 §8.7 ¶4). The library type std::vector takes the place of arrays, . A program creates a vector with new, as any object, and an index outside the vector has no derivation.Without exceptions, the control result of a command is normal or ret v, . No program catches error. Without separate compilation, a program is one translation unit that the type checker sees whole. The preprocessor of Core C++ is a language of its own that runs before the compiler, .\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Design restrictions","header":"9.6. Constructs left out","id":"/Design-restrictions/Constructs-left-out/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions--Constructs-left-out"}});